 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Geometric Baseline Regularization](7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md)
-A composite loss designed to prevent degenerate solutions (e.g., image copying) in few-view settings. It enforces foreground completeness (sum of weights $\approx 1$), frustum consistency (density in multiple views), and depth distribution compactness. Choose this when training on sparse views without external depth maps.
+Geometric Baseline Regularization is a composite loss function designed to prevent degenerate solutions in Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization, particularly in few-view settings where the model might collapse to copying training images. It augments the standard photometric reconstruction loss with three geometric priors: foreground completeness, frustum consistency, and depth distribution compactness. Unlike methods that rely on external depth supervision or semantic embeddings, this approach enforces geometric plausibility directly through the rendered weights and density field properties.
 
 ### [Guided Optimization of NeRF](7496-guided-optimization-of-nerf.md)
-Mitigates shape-radiance ambiguity in indoor scenes by dynamically adjusting the stratified sampling bounds $[t_n, t_f]$ based on adapted monocular depth priors and multi-view consistency errors. Choose this when you have access to monocular depth priors and want to guide sampling without explicit depth loss during training.
+Guided Optimization of NeRF mitigates shape-radiance ambiguity in poorly textured indoor scenes by constraining the sampling range of points along camera rays using adapted monocular depth priors. It sets itself apart from standard NeRF training by dynamically adjusting the near and far bounds of stratified sampling based on multi-view geometric consistency errors, and from [Depth-Supervised NeRF](8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md) by not requiring explicit depth supervision during the optimization loop, instead relying on RGB reconstruction confidence to refine the final depth output.
 
 ### [Depth-Supervised NeRF](8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md)
-Accelerates optimization and improves quality with sparse views by incorporating depth supervision from sparse 3D keypoints (SfM). It minimizes the KL divergence between the rendered ray termination distribution and the noisy depth distribution of keypoints. Choose this when you have sparse 3D keypoints but no dense depth maps.
+Depth-Supervised NeRF (DS-NeRF) is a training procedure that accelerates Neural Radiance Field optimization and improves quality with sparse input views by incorporating depth supervision from sparse 3D keypoints. It solves the problem of NeRF overfitting to incorrect geometries when given insufficient training images by enforcing that the rendered ray termination distribution matches surface priors derived from Structure-from-Motion (SfM). Unlike methods relying on data-driven priors from other scenes or semantic consistency, DS-NeRF requires only the existence of 3D keypoints, making it flexible and independent of training data distribution assumptions.
 
 ### [Robust Depth Loss](9368-robust-depth-loss.md)
-A regularization term using dense but potentially inaccurate pseudo-depth maps. It uses a piecewise function (quadratic for small errors, logarithmic for large errors) to be less sensitive to outliers than standard L2 loss. Choose this when supervising with noisy pseudo-depth labels.
+Robust Depth Loss is a regularization term designed to supervise Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) optimization using dense but potentially inaccurate pseudo-depth maps. It addresses the degradation of extrapolation performance caused by geometry errors in pseudo-labels by being less sensitive to outliers than standard L2 loss. Unlike strict geometric constraints, it encourages rays to terminate near the pseudo-depth while retaining freedom for inaccurate geometry by allowing gradients to vanish for large errors. It differs from [Geometric Baseline Regularization](7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md) by specifically targeting the robustness of depth supervision against noise and outliers rather than enforcing smoothness or baseline consistency.
 
 ### [Depth Structural Consistency Regularization](9371-depth-structural-consistency-regularization.md)
-Enforces continuity constraints on rendered depth maps using external priors (e.g., DepthAnythingV2). It uses a KNN-based hinge loss on local depth variations to preserve sharp edges while smoothing noise. Choose this when you have high-quality external depth priors and need to preserve structural edges.
+Depth Structural Consistency Regularization (RDSC) is a geometric regularization term applied during Neural Radiance Field (NeRF) training to enforce continuity constraints on rendered depth maps, leveraging external depth priors such as those from DepthAnythingV2. It distinguishes itself from sibling methods like [Geometric Baseline Regularization](7475-geometric-baseline-regularization.md) by explicitly modeling local depth variations between neighboring pixels using a k-nearest neighbors (KNN) approach with a boundary threshold, thereby preserving sharp structural edges while smoothing noise.
 
 ### [Novel View Regularization](10806-novel-view-regularization.md)
-Mitigates ambiguity in sparse data by injecting synthetic rays from novel viewpoints into the optimization loop to enforce geometric consistency. Choose this when you lack external priors but want to prevent overfitting to sparse training views.
+Novel View Regularization mitigates the shape-radiance ambiguity in Neural Radiance Fields (NeRF) when training data is sparse by injecting synthetic rays into the optimization loop to provide additional geometric supervision. Unlike methods that rely on external priors or progressive capacity restraint, this approach explicitly guides the network toward geometrically plausible solutions by rendering and supervising views that are not present in the input dataset.
 
 ### [Geometry and Opacity Regularization](11192-geometry-and-opacity-regularization.md)
-Applies explicit constraints to opacity parameters to enforce geometric compactness (sparsity loss) and binary opacity values (entropy loss), reducing "floaters." Choose this when dealing with translucent artifacts or empty space noise.
+Geometry and Opacity Regularization applies explicit constraints to the opacity parameters of a volume rendering representation to enforce geometric compactness and binary opacity values. It solves the problem of "floaters" (translucent artifacts in empty space) and translucent surfaces that degrade the structural fidelity of the reconstructed scene. Unlike sibling methods that rely on external depth priors or structural consistency, this method operates directly on the internal opacity distribution, encouraging sparsity in empty regions and sharp transitions at surfaces.
 
 ### [3D Point Supervision Loss](12693-3d-point-supervision-loss.md)
-Minimizes the L1 distance between learned 3D points (backprojected from rendered depth) and ground-truth 3D points. Choose this for pose refinement in sparse-view regimes where depth-only supervision is insufficient.
+A training loss that minimizes the distance between learned 3D points (obtained by backprojecting rendered depth to the world frame) and ground-truth 3D points, successfully registering poses in sparse-view regimes. Unlike [Depth-Supervised NeRF](8687-depth-supervised-nerf.md), which applies an L1 loss on rendered depth values and struggles to refine poses in sparse settings, 3D Point Supervision enforces a global geometric connection between training images, preventing disjoint local solutions.
 
 ### [Generalized Ray Supervision](14127-generalized-ray-supervision.md)
-Treats arbitrary rays (e.g., shadow rays) as sensors to constrain the density field based on light transport properties, without requiring explicit geometric labels. Choose this when you have physical light transport data (e.g., shadow maps) rather than depth maps.
+Generalized Ray Supervision is a framework for optimizing neural scene representations by treating arbitrary rays in a scene as "ray sensors" that record incoming radiance, extending the standard NeRF approach which is limited to camera rays. It differs from sibling methods like [Depth and Geometry Supervision](8727-depth-and-geometry-supervision.md) by not requiring explicit geometric labels (e.g., depth maps or normals) but instead leveraging the physical light transport properties of specific ray types (such as shadow rays) to constrain the density field.
 
